# libpdx-cap — caps.decl
#
# Wave: R49
# Doc:  design/tooling/r49-r50-plan.md §3.1 in the paideia-os repo.
#
# libpdx-cap is a pure userspace library. cap_pack writes bytes into a
# caller-owned buffer; cap_unpack reads bytes from a caller-owned buffer
# and populates the module's own .bss; cap_manifest_verify reads two
# caller-owned buffers. No syscall is issued from any entry point in
# any milestone through M4, so libpdx-cap requires NO capabilities of
# its own. The wire-format buffers are backed by caps the CALLER holds
# (typically KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT frames per sys_cap_transfer, or the
# InitCap sidecar per src/kernel/core/loader/init_caps.pdx in
# paideia-os).
#
# R90-XREPO.013.M1-002 (#20) addendum — still (none), and why.
#
# src/cap_reconcile.pdx adds the library's first entry point whose
# EVENTUAL body issues a syscall: cap_reconcile_at_exec, a client
# trampoline over SC+ 118 (sys_exec_reconcile_caps). It is annotated
# !{mem, sysreg} @{cap}. That did NOT change this file, for two
# independent reasons, both worth stating so a future reader does not
# "fix" it:
#
#   1. @{cap} is a paideia-as effect-system capability CLASS — it
#      describes what the eventual body touches. A `requires:` item
#      here names a KIND the library must itself HOLD. libpdx-cap
#      holds no cap handle of its own: cap_reconcile_at_exec asks the
#      kernel to act on the CALLER's authority over a child the
#      CALLER forked. A caller that needs a cap to do that declares it
#      in its own caps.decl, exactly as the note below already says.
#
#   2. At this landing the entry point's body is a placeholder and
#      contains no syscall instruction at all — the kernel dispatch
#      arm for SC+ 118 does not exist yet, so the real trampoline
#      would return -ENOSYS anyway. The "no syscall is issued"
#      sentence above is therefore still literally true of the shipped
#      object code.
#
# Reason 1 survives the body swap; reason 2 does not. Revisit this
# file only if libpdx-cap ever comes to hold a cap of its own.
#
# Callers that use libpdx-cap must independently declare in their own
# caps.decl any caps their tool requires. This file exists so the R49
# caps-manifest tooling can consult a uniform declaration for every
# library — the exact analog of libpdx-argv/caps.decl.
requires: (none)

# Declared output schemas — libpdx-cap does not itself emit records
# to a semantic pipe. When callers ask cap_pack to serialize a Cap
# they receive raw wire bytes (see design/architecture.md §2); the
# schema wrapping that goes on the semantic-pipe wire is
# libpdx-semantic-pipe's PdxCap schema, declared there.
declares_output_schemas: (none)
